Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  Final Type  Surgical Pathology Pathology Report   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  OPERATION DATE  28 REMOVED_MONTH 93 ACCESSIONED ON  28 REMOVED_MONTH 93 at 14 54 CLINICAL DATA  65 year old female with recent biopsy  right breast  revealing a mixture of infiltrating ductal and intraductal in situ pathology with neurendocrine differentiation  long history of fibrocystic disease                                                                                      FINAL DIAGNOSIS     BREAST  RIGHT   MODIFIED RADICAL MASTECTOMY  1  THERE IS NO EVIDENCE OF RESIDUAL CARCINOMA  2  FIBROCYSTIC CHANGES WITH EXTENSIVE DENSE FIBROSIS  MICROCYST FORMATION AND FOCAL DUCTAL EPITHELIAL HYPERPLASIA  3  FOREIGN BODY GIANT CELL REACTION TO SUTURE  FAT NECROSIS AND HEMOSIDERIN DEPOSITION CONSISTENT WITH PREVIOUS BIOPSY SITE  4  THERE IS NO EVIDENCE OF MALIGNANCY IN TWELVE OF TWELVE LYMPH NODES  Diagnosis by  Deborah S  Fleischhacker  MD ScD Signed On  04 REMOVED_MONTH 93 at 18 07                                                                                 GROSS DESCRIPTION  Received fresh  labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME  patient s name  unit number  and  Right breast and axilla  is a modified radical mastectomy specimen consisting of a 18 x 9 x 5cm  piece of breast with nipple and overlying skin ellipse  and an 11 x 5 x 1 5cm axillary tail  3cm to the left of the nipple is a 3 5cm linear  healing incision  Beneath this are several REMOVED_DATE 3cm firm nodules  A 10 x REMOVED_DATE x 5cm stellate  hard  white fibrotic area extends to 0 2cm from the deep margin and is surrounded by indurated  fibrous breast tissue  The remaining parenchyma is focally hemorrhagic  The nipple is unremarkable  The axillary nodes are as large as 1 0cm in diameter and are moderately firm and pink  SECTION CODE  A B   representative sections of dense mass C   deep margin D through F   separate nodular areas from beneath nipple G through J   nodes from axillary tail